      "title": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0365a84be70f7e26\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nHypomagnesemia and hypokalemia: a successful oral therapeutic approach after 16 years of potassium and magnesium intravenous replacement therapy - PMC\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"0365a84be70f7e26\">>>",
      "url": "https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4377789/",
      "description": "\n<<<EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9a0e57d994be2556\">>>\nSource: Web Search\n---\nDuring one of her clinic visits in the second year of follow-up, the patient reported that she had switched back to magnesium oxide on her own accord due to the high cost of the magnesium chloride, but this seemed to worsen her diarrhea, and her magnesium levels were low again at 0.45 mmol/L. Following our recommendation, she restarted the magnesium chloride, with subsequent improvement in her diarrhea and an increase in her magnesium level to 0.55 mmol/L. More than 2 years after presenting to us, she had a repeat endoscopy due to her ongoing diarrhea, which, although improved, was still prese\n<<<END_EXTERNAL_UNTRUSTED_CONTENT id=\"9a0e57d994be2556\">>>",
      "siteName": "pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov"
